WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Industrial network traffic is often the broadest OT telemetry source.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:13.800
Passive sensors can identify assets, peers, protocols, timing, and communication across trust boundaries.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:23.100
Protocol-aware telemetry may add operation types, sessions, error conditions, or engineering activity that ordinary flow records cannot explain.

4
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:29.940
HMI and engineering-workstation logs provide endpoint and application context.

5
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:39.960
Useful records may include authentication, process launches, project access, configuration activity, alarms, removable media, and connections to controllers.

6
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:45.100
Collection must be tested so security tooling does not interfere with supported applications.

7
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.980
Historian data can connect a cyber observation to process behavior.

8
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.760
Trends, alarms, and event time can show whether equipment state changed near an alert.

9
00:00:54.860 --> 00:01:05.500
Firewall, VPN, jump-host, and remote-access logs describe boundary connections, identities, approvals, session duration, and destinations.

10
00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:10.660
Identity events add account lifecycle, authentication, and privilege evidence.

11
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:21.480
Controller change records, project repositories, vendor tools, or management platforms may report downloads, mode changes, firmware work, or configuration differences.

12
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.620
These sources are especially valuable when network content is encrypted or proprietary.

13
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.820
Every source has limitations.

14
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:36.920
Passive sensors miss unobserved segments, dormant devices, local actions, and some serial traffic.

15
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.860
Endpoint logs may be unavailable or incomplete.

16
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.000
Historians may show process effects without identifying the initiating user.

17
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.500
Remote systems may use inconsistent clocks or asset names.

18
00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:59.100
Detection engineering should document coverage, blind spots, retention, time synchronization, field quality, and source ownership.

19
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.200
No single feed is the truth.

20
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:10.660
Strong detections combine complementary evidence and make clear what the data can support, what it cannot prove, and how analysts can obtain missing context.
